      "claim": "The spec states: \"None of the five declares a mutant, and the witness gate reports skip for them.\" Reading `saffron/gates/core/witness.py`, the gate does not produce a per-criterion verdict at all - it builds one `declared` list from every criterion that does carry a `mutant` (here, criteria 6 and 7), and returns a single aggregate `GateResult` for the whole spec. Criteria with no mutant are simply never entered into that list; they are not individually reported as `skip`. Given this spec does declare two mutants, the gate's real aggregate status - assuming criteria 6 and 7's witnesses die under their mutants as intended - would be `pass`, not `skip`. The substantive guidance to the implementing agent (you don't owe criteria 1, 2, 3, 5, 8 a mutant) is correct; only the literal description of what status the gate reports is off. This changes nothing about what gets built or how the criteria are tested, so it costs nothing to leave, but it's inaccurate about the current code's behavior at this snapshot.",
